A||||5||Formula||TIMING is everything||||1||
Z||000000||Formula||10-26-2000||07:28 PM||||how in the heck do you time these things, V6 model..... I grounded the computer, set the timing to 10 degrees and it ran like crap, so I moved the dist. back to where it was before (like 40 degrees lol) and it runs fine.  BUT, if I leave it timed like it is now it will backfire (I think its missing) after driving on the highway at 65 mph for 15 mins.  The ignition coil, wires, dist cap and rotor are new and the spark plugs are fine.  The only thing I can think of is the dist needing to be turned 90 degrees.  What do you guys think, and could you explain timing these a little bit, I used the #1 and #4 cylinders and took the avg.||||reg||1
Z||000001||terryk||10-26-2000||07:45 PM||||Don't let the injectors hear you say that...<P>Are you on plug #1? For sure?||||reg||1
Z||000002||btoth||10-27-2000||08:49 AM||||Just a sugestion... but are you sure you lined up the correct timming mark? Both my friend's and my car have 3 or so marks on the belt... Gotta line up the fatest of them with the 10 degree mark. It's easy to get them mixed up if your timming was off far enough.||||reg||1
Z||000003||ThatRickGuy||10-27-2000||09:23 AM||||Just out of curiosity, did you pull the distributer? it's an easy mistake to put it in 180 degrees. other then that, I'd say tripple check your fireing order. the trunk side of the engine from left to right is <BR>5-3-1 and the cabin side left to right is<BR>6-4-2. The dist is 1-6 in order clockwise w/ the number 1 pointed towards the number 4 cylinder. and make sure you're using the correct timing mark, I put a new balancer on mine and I only had one timing mark, but I've heard that the stock ones had 3.||||reg||1
Z||000004||StuGood||10-27-2000||12:37 PM||||btoth took the words right out of my mouth.  Make sure you use the widest of the 3 timing marks on the damper.  That's why I put a swatch of bright orange paint on the damper, on either side of the correct mark.  It makes it very easy to see, and there's never a question which timing mark I'm looking at.<P>Another possibility is, if the elastomer (rubber ring) in the damper is about to let go, it can screw up the indexing of the timing mark.  Might want to check it out, before it shears off completely.   ||||reg||4
Z||000005||Formula||10-27-2000||03:56 PM||||Im sure I used the right cylinders, I did notice that there were 3 timing marks, so I turned it until the distributor rotor was facing towards the #1 cylinder out, and I used that mark, I dont know if it was widest. I didnt drive it today and will probably take it to a shop (NON GM) to get it looked at.   ||||reg||1
